WEBVTT

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:14.440
An AI agent can use tools, such as a web browser, while it works on a task. We'll add a tool to our paper model: sending a text message. It's a way to see who does what when a model asks for help.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:25.880
Here's the rule. Generate from your grid exactly as before. But the moment you roll a punctuation mark, a full stop or a comma, pause. That's a tool call.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:36.140
In our activity, the model rolls punctuation like any other word. Some rows never call the tool. Some always do.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:50.760
Say you've written "here comes the dog". The row for "dog": nought to four is a full stop, five to nine is "runs". Roll a three. Full stop. So pause.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:01:00.140
The tool is a text message. Send "What comes next?", and the sentence so far, to three friends or group chats.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:12.460
The first reply back, "and it wants my sandwich", is your tool result. Write the whole thing down, then the full stop you rolled. Then carry on, from the full stop.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:24.960
Our grid has no row for "sandwich". We restart from the full stop so we have a row to use. That also means the reply cannot change our model's next choice.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:37.700
Replies take time. If nothing's back, leave a gap before the punctuation mark. Write the mark and keep going from its row. Fill the gap when the first reply arrives.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:51.900
The model rolled a full stop after "dog". You paused it, sent the message, and added the reply before carrying on. Those jobs make you the harness: the part that connects the model to its tools.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:02:05.040
Software can do those jobs too. In a larger model, the reply can inform what happens next: a search result might lead to another search. Our paper model always carries on from the punctuation row.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:18.760
What would you let an agent do without asking you first? Pause here and discuss that with your group. Then try the activity, with your group doing the harness's jobs.
